  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-3x-2?$\n\nI. $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x)$\n\nII. $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)$\n\nIII. $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder",
  "worked_solution": "The factor theorem states that $(ax-b)$ is a factor of the polynomial $p(x)$ if and only if the root $x=\\frac{b}{a}$ is also root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering $(3x+2).$ The root of this expression can be found by setting the expression equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each of the statements, in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+2{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-3(-\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =3(-\\frac{8}{27})+2(\\frac{4}{9})+3(\\frac{2}{3})-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{9}+\\frac{8}{9}+2-2 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the factor theorem, since $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ is a root of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $(3x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then $(3x+2)$ divides $f(x)$ with zero remainder.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
